HST Office: the ribbon's priority box lays out only the children it still has.

PriorityHBox (the notebookbar box whose groups give way by priority) collects its prioritized
children once, at its first layout, as plain pointers in m_aSortedChildren, and every later
Resize walks that list. A child disposed since then is gone from the child list but stays in the
list; once it is deleted the pointer is stale. The loop that shows the higher priority children
again read the parent of dynamic_cast<vcl::Window*>(entry) before checking it for null.

That happens when a document is closed from another thread (UNO bridge, automation): the request
thread disposes the notebookbar in SfxFrame::DoClose, a window destroyed there is handed to the
main thread with SendMessage, which releases the SolarMutex, and the main thread handles a
pending resize of the notebookbar. Windows r1-auto W15 at 150% (Writer, print preview, then the
document closed over UNO; the ribbon is narrower than its groups at that scale) died this way
(ProcDump full dump, run 42955213065: main thread WM_SIZE -> VclContainer::setLayoutPosSize ->
ImplCallResize -> PriorityHBox::Resize -> vcl::Window::GetParent with a null this; request
thread in SwXTextDocument::close -> SfxFrame::DoClose -> VCLXWindow::dispose ->
SfxToolBoxControl::disposing -> InterimItemWindow::dispose -> ComboBox::dispose -> SendMessage). The office's own crash handler then faulted as well, so
the office exited with 255 and left no Windows dump.

Resize now touches an entry only when it is still one of the box's current children (compared
by address, the entry itself is not read) and checks the cast. Inherited from upstream (same
code in 26.8).

